"""Approved replacements and their interpretations; applied after base report text."""
def apply(figures,why,more,captions):
 by={f['figure']:f for f in figures}
 changes={
 4:dict(path='output/pcp_changes/pcp_changes.png',title='Similar internet gains, different infrastructure changes.',extension_key='paired_change_pcp',
 question='Do countries with similar gains in internet use also have similar changes in their telecom indicators?',
 encoding='Each line connects one country\'s four changes from 2015 to 2020. At every axis, change is calculated as the 2020 value minus its 2015 value. Internet use changes in percentage points; the other axes show changes in subscriptions per 100 people. All 175 matched countries remain visible. Blue highlights the 39 countries that gained 20-30 percentage points in internet use; gray retains the other 136. Three selected examples are named and colored separately. The dashed horizontal line aligns zero on all four axes: above it means an increase and below it a decrease. Each axis has its own stated range.',
 rationale='The annual paths in Figure 2 compare internet use and broadband for six examples. This parallel-coordinate view connects all four changes for the full matched sample, so one country can be followed across several measures. Brushing a common range of internet gains asks what else changed among countries making broadly similar progress. Thin background lines retain the full sample, while three named records make the selected pattern easier to trace. The browser supports brushing, axis reordering, hover values and a straight-versus-gently-bundled comparison.',
 interpretation='The highlighted countries gain between 20 and 30 percentage points in internet use, yet their lines spread apart on the infrastructure axes. Among these 39 countries, fixed-broadband change ranges from about -0.3 to +16.6 subscriptions per 100 people, landline change from -14.8 to +7.1, and mobile change from -80.2 to +34.5. Similar internet gains therefore occur alongside different combinations of reported telecom changes. The selection is a stated comparison range, not a country category discovered by an algorithm.\n\nGeorgia and Brunei make this distinction easy to follow. Internet use rises by about 25.0 points in Georgia and 25.1 in Brunei. Their fixed broadband also rises, by about 8.7 and 7.7 subscriptions per 100 respectively. The remaining axes separate them: Georgia loses about 14.8 landline and 12.0 mobile subscriptions per 100, while Brunei gains about 5.2 landline and 10.2 mobile subscriptions per 100. Even similar gains in both internet use and fixed broadband can hide changes in opposite directions on the other two measures.\n\nUzbekistan provides another selected example. Internet use rises by 28.3 points, alongside gains of about 10.0 fixed-broadband, 7.1 landline and 28.6 mobile subscriptions per 100. Its line stays above zero on every axis. Compare this with Georgia, whose internet and broadband gains sit above zero while its landline and mobile changes fall below it. The common zero position makes these direction differences visible without treating the four units as interchangeable.\n\nThis supports the change-over-time part of our main question: one adoption gain can hide several different changes in the other indicators. A 2020 snapshot cannot recover those paired changes. Figure 5 answers the separate question of what the country profiles look like at that endpoint; Figure 7 checks how the overall associations differ between levels and gains. This PCP adds the individual four-measure combinations behind those summaries.\n\nRead exact changes at the axis intersections. The 20% bundle gently bends selected and remaining records toward their respective average paths between axes, while preserving the measured intersections. The curves between axes are drawing aids. Five-year differences also omit the annual timing inside the interval. Subscription changes are not counts of unique people, and these comparisons do not show which infrastructure change caused the internet-use gain.',
 note='The selected gain range is descriptive. Different axis ranges preserve a shared zero position, but equal heights or slopes do not mean equal changes in the four units.',
 takeaway='Georgia and Brunei both gain about 25 percentage points in internet use and around eight fixed-broadband subscriptions per 100 people. Yet landline and mobile subscriptions fall in Georgia and rise in Brunei. Across the 39 selected countries, the four changes form many different combinations. Similar internet progress therefore does not describe one common infrastructure history; keeping each country connected across the axes reveals what a single gain or endpoint would hide.',
 caption='Paired 2015-2020 changes for 175 countries; 39 with 20-30-point internet-use gains are selected, with Georgia, Brunei and Uzbekistan highlighted.'),
 5:dict(path='output/internet_revision/pcp_gentle_bundles.png',title='Gentle bundles reveal different country profiles.',extension_key='gentle_pcp',
 question='Do countries with high internet use share the same infrastructure profile?',
 encoding='Each curve is one of the same 175 countries in 2020. The four vertical axes retain their original units and full matched-country ranges. Orange means below 40% internet use, blue means 40-below-70%, and green means at least 70%. Within each chosen band, curves bend gently toward the average profile between neighboring axes. The 35% setting preserves every measured axis intersection and keeps each bend between its two endpoint values. These bands are chosen comparison groups, not discovered country types.',
 rationale='Parallel coordinates keep four measurements connected for each country. Thin curves and three consistent colors make the full set easier to follow without replacing records with filled ribbons. Figure 4 provides a close view of six exceptions; this figure restores the full-country context. The browser lets the reader compare straight and bundled paths, brush ranges, reorder axes and inspect country values.',
 interpretation='Start on the internet-use axis and follow the green curves toward fixed broadband. High-adoption countries occupy a wide range of broadband values, including countries close to zero. Figure 4 identifies six specific countries with at least 70% internet use and no more than five fixed-broadband subscriptions per 100. Thus, a shared adoption band does not imply one infrastructure profile.\n\nOn the mobile axis, curves from different adoption bands overlap. Mobile subscriptions alone therefore do not neatly separate low- and high-adoption countries. The matrix in Figure 6 checks these relationships more precisely. Curves help trace records, but their apparent height or slope between axes has no measured meaning. Each axis has a different scale, so compare intersections and labelled values rather than the steepness of a curve.\n\nThis answers the relationships part of our main question: a summary such as high adoption can hide different combinations of the other indicators. The browser preserves each country for inspection instead of treating the colored bands as identical members.',
 note='Adoption bands are descriptive choices. Only axis intersections are observations; bundling does not establish causes.',
 takeaway='The high-adoption band includes countries with very different fixed-broadband and landline values, while mobile values overlap across adoption bands. One adoption label therefore hides several infrastructure profiles. Gentle bundling helps follow the records, but exact comparisons still come from the axis values and browser selection.',
 caption='175 matched country profiles in 2020, with 35% curve bundling and three stated adoption bands; original axis values are preserved.'),
 7:dict(path='output/proposals_20261026/recommended_fig7_association_audit.png',title='Country levels and country gains tell different stories.',extension_key='association_audit',
 question='Does a strong relationship between country levels also hold for gains within the same countries?',
 encoding='The left panel compares Spearman rank correlations with internet use for fixed broadband, landlines and mobile. Blue dots use 2020 country levels; orange dots use each country\'s 2020-minus-2015 gains. Each gray line joins the two correlations for one measure. The right panel shows the actual broadband and adoption gains, with one dot per country. Both comparisons use the same 175 complete country pairs. Correlation describes how consistently two variables increase together across countries.',
 rationale='Figure 6 shows relationships between country levels. Repeating the comparison after calculating each country\'s gain tests whether that snapshot relationship carries over to progress. The paired-dot chart compares all three measures without repeating six scatterplots. The broadband-gain scatterplot keeps the underlying observations visible, so the coefficients can be checked against the shape of the data.',
 interpretation='For 2020 levels, the broadband-adoption correlation is about 0.83. Countries with more fixed broadband generally also have higher internet use. When we compare gains within the same countries, that value falls to about 0.05. The right panel shows why: countries with similar broadband gains have widely different adoption gains, rather than forming a clear upward pattern.\n\nThe distinction is not limited to broadband. Landlines fall from about 0.79 for levels to 0.29 for gains, and mobile falls from 0.53 to 0.07. All three relationships are weaker after changing the question from which countries have more to which countries gained more. The same country set is retained, so a change in sample membership does not explain the contrast.\n\nThis directly answers the snapshots part of our main question. A strong relationship in one year cannot be used as evidence that the same infrastructure gain accompanied adoption progress within countries. This is a descriptive comparison, not proof that infrastructure has no effect: rank correlation alone does not establish causes, account for earlier investment, or model delayed effects. The selected country paths in Figure 2 show some of the different histories behind this result.',
 note='Gain correlations describe differences in five-year changes across the paired countries; they are not correlations of every annual observation within a country.',
 takeaway='Broadband and adoption have a strong relationship in 2020 levels, about 0.83, but almost no rank relationship between their 2015-2020 gains, about 0.05. Landline and mobile relationships also weaken for gains. A snapshot can show which countries have more, while leaving their different paths of progress hidden.',
 caption='Level and gain correlations for the same 175 countries, with the underlying broadband-adoption gain scatterplot.'),
 10:dict(path='output/proposals_20261026/recommended_fig10_fixed_cohorts.png',title='Did countries that started behind catch up?',extension_key='fixed_initial_cohorts',
 question='Did the countries with the lowest initial internet use catch up as overall adoption rose?',
 encoding='The 159 countries with complete 2000-2020 records are ranked once by their internet use in 2000 and divided into three groups of 53. Orange is the lowest starting third, blue the middle third and purple the highest third. Membership stays fixed in every year. The left panel follows each group\'s yearly median. The right panel shows every country\'s 2020 value; the thick bar spans the middle half and the white circle marks the median. Small vertical offsets separate overlapping country dots and have no data meaning.',
 rationale='Yearly distributions can improve even when the countries that started behind remain behind. Keeping the same initial groups follows those countries instead of replacing them with whichever countries occupy a percentile later. The median paths show the group contrast over time; the individual dots prevent a group summary from hiding exceptions. This complements the later ridgelines, which compare overall and regional distributions.',
 interpretation='The lowest-starting group improves from a median of about 0.2% internet use in 2000 to 36.1% in 2020. The middle group moves from 2.2% to 69.9%, and the highest group from 16.4% to 89.9%. All three groups improve substantially, yet their median levels remain far apart at the endpoint.\n\nThe gap between the highest- and lowest-starting medians grows from 16.2 to 53.8 percentage points. Thus, broad progress does not mean that the initially low-adoption countries catch up in absolute percentage-point terms. The contrast is not caused by moving countries between groups: the original members remain together. A supporting check using four and five initial groups also finds a wider endpoint gap. This concerns an absolute gap; it does not imply that every relative inequality measure worsens.\n\nThe right panel adds an important qualification. Some initially low-adoption countries finish above countries in the higher-starting groups. Initial group membership is therefore not a prediction for every country. The dots show both persistent group differences and individual exceptions.\n\nThis answers the inequality-and-change part of our main question: one improving overall summary can hide unequal progress among the same countries. Countries have equal weight here. These are comparisons of national adoption rates, not population-weighted estimates of the world\'s internet users, and they do not identify why countries progressed differently.',
 note='Starting groups are chosen descriptive categories. A median path is a group summary, not the history of a single representative country.',
 takeaway='All three fixed starting groups improve, but the lowest-starting group reaches a 2020 median of 36.1%, compared with 89.9% for the highest-starting group. Their absolute median gap widens from 16.2 to 53.8 percentage points. Overall progress therefore hides persistent differences, while the individual dots show that some countries cross the group pattern.',
 caption='Fixed 2000 adoption thirds, 53 countries each: yearly medians and individual 2020 country values with interquartile ranges.')}
 for n,c in changes.items():
  f=by[n];f.update(path=c['path'],title=c['title'],extension_key=c['extension_key'],caption=c['caption'],explanation=[c['question'],c['encoding'],c['rationale'],c['note'],c['takeaway']],detailed_interpretation=c['interpretation']);why[n]=c['rationale'];more[n]=c['interpretation'];captions[n]=c['caption']
 f=by[5]
 f['explanation'][2]=f['explanation'][2].replace('Figure 4 provides a close view of six exceptions; this figure restores the full-country context.','Figure 4 compares five-year changes; this figure compares the country profiles at the 2020 endpoint. The two PCPs therefore ask different questions using the same matched countries.')
 f['detailed_interpretation']=f['detailed_interpretation'].replace('Figure 4 identifies six specific countries with at least 70% internet use and no more than five fixed-broadband subscriptions per 100.','For example, Kuwait has about 99.1% internet use but only 1.7 fixed-broadband subscriptions per 100 people. Kyrgyzstan has 72.0% internet use and 4.3 fixed-broadband subscriptions per 100.')
 why[5]=f['explanation'][2];more[5]=f['detailed_interpretation']
 by[7]['detailed_interpretation']=by[7]['detailed_interpretation'].replace('The selected country paths in Figure 2 show some of the different histories behind this result.','The annual paths in Figure 2 retain timing for selected countries. Figure 4 keeps four changes connected for each country, showing the different combinations behind this overall result.')
 by[6]['detailed_interpretation']=by[6]['detailed_interpretation'].replace('Figure 7 moves from those levels to a direct count of matched-country changes.','Figure 7 directly compares the level and gain relationships for all three infrastructure measures.')
